articles.description:
After understanding the Playwright Test Runner and its configuration in Day 2, Day 3 is all about writing effective tests using Playwright’s powerful API. Whether you’re validating elements, simulating user flows, or verifying data, knowing the core syntax is key to writing readable and robust tests.
Test Structure and Basic Syntax
ts
import { test, expect } from '@playwright/test';
test('basic test example', async ({ page }) => {
https://example.com');
Key Components:
expect(): Assertion library for validations.
page: The main browser tab/context used in tests.
Commonly Used Playwright Functions
1. Navigating and Waiting
await page.goto('https://example.com');
2. Interacting with Elements
await page.click('text=Login');
3. Assertions with expect()
await expect(page).toHaveURL(/dashboard/);
Working with Locators
ts
const loginButton = page.locator('button:has-text("Login")');
Locator Tips:
Prefer text-based or role-based selectors for clarity.
page.getByRole(), page.getByLabel(), and page.getByText() are also supported.
Handling Multiple Elements
const items = page.locator('.list-item');
